# TELKOMAS BRANCH CODE FOUNDATION (REVISION-TELKOMAS-BRANCH-CODE-TKM1-TO-TLK1-1)

**Cabang Telkomas' canonical branch code is `TLK1`. `TKM1` is DEPRECATED.**

This rule SUPERSEDES every earlier statement that Telkomas' canonical code is
`TKM1` — including the registry line in rule
`73-rme-branch-sun4-perawat-online-context.mdc` and the corresponding block in
`CLAUDE.md`. Where an older rule, sprint record, wave record or audit entry
mentions `TKM1`, it is a record of the code **at that time** and stays as
written; it is not a current instruction.

## The rules

1. **One branch identity, one canonical code.** `TKM1` and `TLK1` name the SAME
   branch — the same `mst_branches` row, the same id, the same patients, the same
   history. Only the emitted token changed. The branch primary key never moves.

2. **`TLK1` is the only code the application EMITS.** New patients, new Nomor RM
   values, new visit numbers, new rollout declarations, new reports, new exports,
   new fixtures and new seed data all carry `TLK1`. No code path may generate
   `TKM1`.

3. **`TKM1` is ACCEPTED on input, never re-emitted.** It is recognised because it
   is printed on cards already in patients' wallets, on documents already
   archived, and stored in identifiers already issued. Accepting it is a
   compatibility guarantee, not a second canonical form.

4. **The mapping lives in exactly one place:**
   `App\Modules\Branch\Support\BranchCodeAlias`. Never write
   `$code === 'TKM1' || $code === 'TLK1'` at a call site. Call
   `BranchCodeAlias::canonicalize()` before looking a code up or comparing it,
   and `equivalentCodes()` / `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::equivalentNumbers()`
   when matching a STORED value that may hold either spelling.

5. **Nomor RM.** New Telkomas numbers are `DG-TLK1-{TAHUN}-{NOMOR}`.
   `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::branchCodeFrom()` reports the CANONICAL
   code (`DG-TKM1-…` → `TLK1`), so every consumer compares one spelling;
   `literalBranchCodeFrom()` returns what the number literally says, for the
   places where the historical fact is the point.

6. **Transform via the parser, never via string replacement.** Only the
   branch-code SEGMENT may change. `canonicalizeBranchCode()` preserves the
   prefix, the year and the manual sequence verbatim, returns null for anything
   that is not `DG-{KODE}-{TAHUN}-{NOMOR}`, and leaves a `TKM1` occurring inside
   a manual sequence (`DG-LDK2-2024-TKM1`) untouched. A
   `replace(col,'TKM1','TLK1')` over production data is FORBIDDEN.

7. **Exact-token matching, always.** `TKM` is not `TKM1`; `TKM1-EXTRA` is neither;
   `XXTKM1YY` is a different branch code that merely contains the substring and
   is never rewritten. Aliasing widens nothing.

8. **FAIL CLOSED.** An unknown, ambiguous, inactive or non-RME branch code
   resolves to nothing and is refused. There is no
   `unknown → Telkomas` fallback, and no fuzzy or typo-tolerant matching.

9. **Historical Nomor RM must keep resolving.** A patient whose stored number was
   migrated to `DG-TLK1-…` is still found when staff type the `DG-TKM1-…` on their
   old card. This is done by widening the SEARCH
   (`CrossBranchPatientLookupService`, `LegacyRmePatientResolutionAuditService`),
   never by creating a second patient row and never by an alias record.

10. **The source Nomor RM on a legacy document is transcribed FAITHFULLY.**
    `LegacyRmeSourceRmNormalizer` folds transcription noise (case, spacing, dash
    variants) and deliberately does NOT canonicalize the branch code: the
    document really does say `TKM1`, and the archive's job is to say so.
    Reachability comes from alias-aware matching downstream.

11. **Immutable records are never rewritten to remove `TKM1`:** published legacy
    RME / odontogram evidence and its stored source RM, file bytes and checksums;
    `sys_audit_logs`; terminal (COMPLETED / CANCELLED) rollout waves; issued
    `trx_clinic_visits.visit_number` values; and staging rows recording what a
    past import generated. An audit trail that is edited to look tidy is false.

12. **Migration is collision-safe and fails closed.** Renaming the branch code or
    a patient's Nomor RM aborts the whole transaction if the canonical value is
    already held by a different branch or a different patient. Never overwrite,
    never merge patients, never delete a duplicate, never auto-renumber — a
    collision is a master-data decision for a human.

13. **Rollout allowlists canonicalize both sides.** `config/legacy_rme_rollout.php`
    and `LegacyRmeBranchAdmissionService` canonicalize declared tokens as well as
    the resolved branch code, so a deployment whose environment still names the
    deprecated code cannot silently lock an approved branch out of its own wave.

14. **`RmeBranchSeeder` must never create a duplicate Telkomas.** It matches every
    equivalent code before deciding a branch is missing. Converting a deprecated
    code to the canonical one is the migration's job, not the seeder's.

15. **Branch identity is still never taken from a request.** This revision changes
    a label; it does not weaken branch authority. `origin_branch_id` stays
    RM-derived, and `BranchContext` stays the server-side authority.

## Examples

```text
DG-TLK1-2024-9985      canonical
DG-TKM1-2024-9985      HISTORICAL / DEPRECATED ALIAS — accepted, never issued

canonicalize('TKM1')   → 'TLK1'
canonicalize('TLK1')   → 'TLK1'
canonicalize('TKM')    → 'TKM'    (unknown → fails closed at branch lookup)
canonicalize('ZZZ9')   → 'ZZZ9'   (unknown → fails closed at branch lookup)
```
